 Find out more at SatoriSatori.com.

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:18.170
 Tim Page runs the Farmers' Exchange of Earthly Delights, also known as Feed Sonoma, a produce distribution company that works very closely with 50 small-scale farmers in Sonoma County, California.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:22.870
 Tim grew up in Orange County, where he witnessed the disappearance of farmlands firsthand.

00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:31.570
 And that inspired Feed's dedication to creating a food system with efficient practices, pristine raw ingredients, and all while practicing the maximization of our resources.

00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:40.410
 Chelsea talks with Tim about the origins of this business under an oak tree, supporting a community of farmers, and what to do with the ridiculous abundance of California.

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:46.240
 Thank you for having me, and thank you for having Feed Sonoma.

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:57.730
 It's really an honor, because I feel like we're in such a time where it's so important to have a community of people coming together to share stories and tell stories of what's happening in our community.

00:02:57.870 --> 00:02:58.850
 So thank you for doing that.

00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:04.570
 Well, maybe let's start with when did Feed start, and how did you get here?

00:03:04.790 --> 00:03:05.770
 Yeah, great question.

00:03:06.350 --> 00:03:17.140
 I mean, how I got here, that could take up the whole 45 minutes or an hour, because, I mean, I think that's what we have here in these types of communities of multiple small groups of people coming together.

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:27.460
 You have such a wide array of stories of each individual, how they got to where they are, and we all have such a unique evolution, and mine kind of fits that mold.

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:39.910
 But as far as Feed Sonoma is concerned, Feed is actually, we've been in existence since June 1st of 2011, but in reality, the whole, the business has been in continuous operation since 1979.

00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:50.910
 And it's started, it's always been like a real mom-and-pop grassroots, you know, basically just like a, started in 1979, it was called Ocean Resources International for some reason.

00:03:51.130 --> 00:04:02.520
 I'm not sure why the name, but a woman named Ina Chun, and here in West County, West Sebastopol, and the legend has it, because I still haven't ever met her, I would like to someday, because she is still living in the area.

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:20.950
 But the legend has it that the produce used to come in, and there was like maybe a dozen farmers, let's say, that she was working with, and she just had connections with chefs in the Bay Area that were, you know, 1979, you're talking probably not that many folks that were just starting to get into this whole idea of supporting small local farmers and a farm-to-table movement.

00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:32.440
 But like Alice Waters and Bob Klein and that whole crew, but they used to gather the produce under an oak tree, and then on a Thursday afternoon, no refrigeration, nothing.

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:40.360
 And then on Thursday night, they'd go drive down to the city and deliver everything, because they'd either get there before the restaurant closed, or they had keys, and they could drop it off or whatever.

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:55.720
 But anyway, then she sold the business to a couple, Susan Stover and Tony Sidoti, and they were growing specialty greens and herbs for Ina and for that business, and Ina just wanted to move on.

00:04:55.880 --> 00:05:03.870
 And so they, Susan and Tony bought the business from them, but never was really an intention of, you know, being part of this food movement that we have now.

00:05:04.010 --> 00:05:09.750
 It was more just they wanted to continue and wanted to support local farmers that were growing beautiful product.

00:05:09.890 --> 00:05:18.080
 And obviously, as you know, when you get to be in the midst of such beautiful food that is artisanally grown, you become almost addicted to it.

00:05:18.220 --> 00:05:21.500
 And just to be surrounded by it is kind of like gives you this buzz, you know.

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:27.770
 And so I think they just wanted to be part of that, and they're obviously looking to just survive like any of us are doing as well.

00:05:27.870 --> 00:05:42.820
 But Susan and Tony stewarded the business from 1994 until 2011, and then when they were just looking to move on as well, you know, driving a delivery truck around San Francisco can be stressful and a little laborious.

00:05:43.250 --> 00:05:58.320
 But they were looking to move on, and just so happened at that particular point in time, for my personal self, I was kind of looking to be able to create something that felt like it was going to be meaningful to be able to steward to the next generation.

00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:04.240
 I had just, you know, become a father, basically, and was starting a family of my own.

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:14.870
 And I met a friend named Michelle Dubin, who is a small farmer here in West Sonoma County.

00:06:15.390 --> 00:06:30.520
 And she had been going to these kind of food advocacy meetings, the Food Systems Alliance and that type of thing, and really was just kind of wanting to help give small farmers an ability to sell more food.

00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:40.980
 And so we kind of got together the winter of 2010 going into 2011 and started to formulate how we could create a business that would help small farmers sell more food.

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:45.440
 And then lo and behold, we discovered that Susan and Tony were selling their business, which was called Terrace.

00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:47.560
 It had now become Terrace Sonoma Produce.

00:06:48.380 --> 00:06:57.360
 And so Michelle and I partnered up and basically purchased that business from Susan and Tony, and then that became Feed Sonoma on June 1st of 2011.

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:06.640
 So, you know, we have this amazing foundation that we have, you know, really focusing on the quality of how the food is grown.

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:19.500
 You know, I hear the term like ecologically sound and, you know, really supporting that because in our type of area, we have, you know, with this type of a farm community where the average size farm is maybe 10 acres, let's say.

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:29.980
 And there's a lot of direct contact between small farms and individuals, maybe at farm markets or CSAs, then not every farm is going to be certified organic.

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:46.400
 But also all the farms that we work with are either certified organic, which is only about 45 percent of the farms, 40 percent of the farms, or they're just what we call beyond organic, which is this kind of nebulous term that gets used a lot because it doesn't really have an auditable definition.

00:07:47.090 --> 00:07:53.340
 But we just basically choose to support farms that are growing and are at least using organic practices.

00:07:53.860 --> 00:08:10.900
 And pretty much all of the farms that we work with are also incorporating farm practices that we would deem beyond organic, such as, you know, making a point of planting flowers that attract beneficial insects or, you know, rotating crops or, you know, making their own compost or cover cropping or whatever.

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:12.720
 I mean, I know you know all this stuff.

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:15.360
 And so, but that's the main focus of what we do.

00:08:15.440 --> 00:08:17.620
 We don't work with farms that use chemicals or GMOs.

00:08:17.620 --> 00:08:19.640
 And so that's kind of the focus of where we are.

00:08:19.720 --> 00:08:22.970
 But yeah, it started in, basically, feed started on June 1st, 2011.

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:35.920
 Well, I would love to come back and talk about what those farming practices are, because I think that's actually a really interesting part of working with farmers on what a definition of an ecologically sound farm is.

00:08:36.920 --> 00:08:39.340
 But first, I want to know, how many farms do you work with?

00:08:39.700 --> 00:08:40.790
 Yeah, good question.

00:08:40.790 --> 00:08:57.650
 So, and I'll go back to June 1st, 2011, when, when we took over, and I kind of came in and just took over all the day to day operations, basically, literally was receiving all the produce and labeling all the produce and loading the truck and driving the truck down and making deliveries and all that.

00:08:57.650 --> 00:09:06.550
 But we started back then with about 15 farms and about, we had one truck, 15 farms, one employee, basically, being me.

00:09:07.110 --> 00:09:13.160
 And working with about maybe 25 clients, buyers, you know, it's all wholesale.

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:15.600
 So, chefs, caterers, markets.

00:09:16.640 --> 00:09:22.710
 But now, over the course of a year now, like in 2015, we'll work with a little over 60 farms.

00:09:23.900 --> 00:09:27.600
 Like, I think right now on our website, we have 62 farms listed on there.

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:32.700
 But that's, it kind of always changes, you know, in our type of zone, too, because of the size of our farms.

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:41.120
 You know, a couple of farmers might just decide to move on, or maybe they lost their land because there's this leasehold land issue, you know, for young farmers in our community.

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:46.150
 Or, obviously, on the positive side, we do have new farmers that are coming into the community as well.

00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:57.840
 So, yeah, so now, at the end of 2015, we have three going on four trucks, you know, about 15 employees, you know, a lot of them part-time, and about 60 farms, basically.

00:09:58.020 --> 00:10:02.910
 And then, I would say on the buy side, you know, consistently maybe like 85 buyers.

00:10:03.010 --> 00:10:11.260
 So, we're not a huge business by any means, but all the growth has come organically, meaning within the community, you know, really haven't done any sales or marketing.

00:10:12.290 --> 00:10:13.730
 You know, exercises at all.

00:10:13.730 --> 00:10:20.760
 It's just been word of mouth, really, within the chef community, people telling their friends about where they're getting their awesome food.

00:10:20.860 --> 00:10:29.060
 And then, of course, within the farm community, if a farmer feels like it's working using a model like Feed Sonoma, then, of course, that kind of spreads as well.

00:10:30.010 --> 00:10:38.960
 Well, I know about you from Doralees at the cheese shop in Healdsburg, and I was a CSA member there for a while, and it was an amazing CSA.

00:10:39.180 --> 00:10:40.240
 So, I'm a big fan.

00:10:40.660 --> 00:10:52.160
 But let's talk about what a normal day looks like here, where, like, how does the produce get here, what happens when it shows up, and then how does it get to these, you know, two-year accounts?

00:10:52.420 --> 00:10:52.660
 Yeah.

00:10:53.680 --> 00:10:54.580
 You know, it's interesting.

00:10:54.700 --> 00:11:09.760
 It's actually, we generally have two, I don't know how many days there are, but we have two cycles through the week, where it's everything that we do is harvest to order, meaning that what we're doing is aggregating information from the farmers with what, about what they have in the field to harvest.

00:11:10.770 --> 00:11:17.180
 And then we, you know, put that information into a list, send that out to all the buyers, you know, all the chefs and market buyers.

00:11:18.300 --> 00:11:20.790
 So, for instance, today's Friday.

00:11:20.930 --> 00:11:22.410
 We have trucks making deliveries today.

00:11:22.590 --> 00:11:26.430
 Our next cycle is going to be Monday harvest for Tuesday delivery.

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:35.540
 So, like, literally today, tomorrow, I'll be getting information from the farm community about what they have to harvest for feed on Monday.

00:11:36.260 --> 00:11:47.800
 We'll send out a list midday, Sunday, and then it's this really quick cycle where by Sunday night, you know, the deadline is 4 in the morning on Monday, but Sunday night, all of our buyers will be placing their orders.

00:11:48.260 --> 00:11:53.000
 So that first thing Monday morning, we can give the pick list to all the farms.

00:11:53.540 --> 00:11:58.680
 And it's usually, like, a majority pick list because there's always going to be things that change throughout the morning on Monday.

00:12:00.230 --> 00:12:06.900
 So once that happens, the farms are out harvesting and packing and organizing and getting all of the produce ready.

00:12:07.460 --> 00:12:16.780
 And then the majority of farms will actually then, in the afternoon and evening, will deliver their food here to our warehouse, our little hub here.

00:12:17.440 --> 00:12:22.220
 And then we also have one truck that goes out and picks up produce from a handful of farms as well.

00:12:22.660 --> 00:12:23.680
 So the produce comes in.

00:12:23.780 --> 00:12:25.520
 It's coming in farm-specific.

00:12:25.820 --> 00:12:27.120
 Everything's coming in per farm.

00:12:27.160 --> 00:12:36.510
 And then within the warehouse here, everything then gets kind of organized and turned into a buyer-specific, put on pallets and getting ready to be put on trucks.

00:12:36.810 --> 00:12:40.790
 And then basically that will happen on Monday night.

00:12:40.970 --> 00:12:47.320
 And then Tuesday morning, our first driver shows up here at, like, 2.30 in the morning and takes off, and he heads down to San Francisco.

00:12:47.940 --> 00:12:55.680
 And then our other two drivers will come in at about 4, and they'll help each other load their trucks.

00:12:55.820 --> 00:13:01.160
 And the second truck goes down to Marin in the East Bay, and then the third truck stays here in Sonoma County.

00:13:01.580 --> 00:13:03.000
 And that's kind of how it happens.

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:14.870
 And my role in that is to obviously spread the word and kind of, you know, get people excited and kind of do my best, even though we're all kind of maxed out.

00:13:14.970 --> 00:13:18.170
 But, you know, tell a few stories here and there and get people excited about what's happening.

00:13:18.770 --> 00:13:26.200
 But also, main role for me is on Monday morning, this coming Monday, is kind of coordinating the harvest, if you will.

00:13:26.660 --> 00:13:35.340
 Like, basically, because all the information is coming in, if there's any edits to an order, or if a chef wants to add something to the order, or whatever it might be, that information has to be translated.

00:13:35.560 --> 00:13:46.150
 So, you use this word micro-distributor, and I don't really know exactly how a regular produce distributor works.

00:13:46.330 --> 00:13:51.610
 So, maybe you could talk about what's different with your model and what regular produce distribution looks like.

00:13:51.870 --> 00:13:52.610
 Yeah, for sure.

00:13:53.330 --> 00:13:57.840
 Well, I'll just say that we actually call ourselves like a micro-regional.

00:13:58.880 --> 00:14:02.310
 We call it micro-regional produce aggregation and distribution.

00:14:02.630 --> 00:14:10.970
 So, it's this long-winded term, but micro-regional, really, we use that term because we're focusing on a distinct micro-region, such as Sonoma County.

00:14:10.970 --> 00:14:26.790
 So, really, it stems from the idea that creating a very vibrant and efficient food system moving forward is going to be about kind of tapping into what indigenous peoples have always done, which is honoring their particular watershed.

00:14:27.980 --> 00:14:40.190
 And so, as a food system, when you do that, you can basically focus on the areas that have the ability to support growing maybe perishable greens, let's say.

00:14:40.270 --> 00:14:43.090
 Like, you have the soil type and the climate and the water availability.

00:14:43.500 --> 00:14:51.260
 And that generally is going to be in kind of concentrated areas, almost like if you look at a relief map, you know, where you'd have the green, basically.

00:14:51.260 --> 00:15:12.130
 And so, focusing on particular micro-regions within those areas as a food system, but also feed Sonoma, which is the function of aggregation from the farms and distribution to a consumer, that is simply going to mirror that kind of biological function.

00:15:12.800 --> 00:15:20.450
 And so, that's our goal is to create this really efficient business model since we have to operate within these current constructs of our modern society.

00:15:20.450 --> 00:15:29.840
 So, we have to have a business that can pay its bills, but to conduct business in a way that actually can mirror the organic biological function.

00:15:30.820 --> 00:15:46.110
 So, and then also in doing that, now, once again, since I don't have like all these scientific studies and I, you know, can't be quoted on all these different facts or whatever, but I like to, I do like to think that in using our own intuition that we all have.

00:15:46.110 --> 00:16:00.080
 It's like, I, for me personally, I just think we know that we become more efficient and more abundant when we focus on a community level and then figure out a way to have those communities work together.

00:16:01.490 --> 00:16:10.400
 And so, from a business standpoint, that allows us to be really efficient because we can have a, with this harvest order model, we don't have to inventory product, for instance.

00:16:10.940 --> 00:16:15.080
 So, because everything that a buyer orders is getting harvested for that specific order.

00:16:15.560 --> 00:16:22.870
 And so, when you do that, you have a smaller infrastructure here at our warehouse, you know, less cold storage because you don't have to keep stuff for long periods of time.

00:16:24.110 --> 00:16:34.270
 So, basically, you can create the infrastructure of this function that is Feed Sonoma for this farm community that is more efficient, you know, it has less overhead, less smaller carbon footprint and all that.

00:16:34.390 --> 00:16:36.920
 And so, you know, you don't have to get 20 trucks.

00:16:36.960 --> 00:16:39.940
 You can probably figure out how to be really dynamic with a smaller scale.

00:16:40.100 --> 00:16:45.370
 Like, right now, we're going to get our fourth truck in the spring, and I don't really see needing to go beyond that.

00:16:45.490 --> 00:16:51.190
 You know, once we kind of have that in place, then we just look at maybe adding another harvest and delivery day and kind of using that.

00:16:51.190 --> 00:16:55.810
 And then, knowing that we're at the tip of the iceberg is a food community here in the Bay Area.

00:16:55.910 --> 00:16:59.230
 I mean, we have an amazing community of farmers and people that support those farmers.

00:16:59.350 --> 00:16:59.890
 Don't get me wrong.

00:17:00.610 --> 00:17:11.890
 But we also vastly, if we were actually able to guarantee that all the farmers were actually selling all their food, which we still don't even do, even though the supply is so far below the potential demand.

00:17:12.880 --> 00:17:20.990
 But even if we maxed out what the farmers are doing right now, we know that we only are growing maybe 10% of the food that's actually purchased in Sonoma County.

00:17:20.990 --> 00:17:22.510
 So we're at the tip of the iceberg, basically.

00:17:23.670 --> 00:17:26.270
 So anyway, that's kind of another long story.

00:17:26.430 --> 00:17:31.190
 But that's generally what micro-regional produce distribution is to me.

00:17:31.610 --> 00:17:49.230
 I think, you know, I don't necessarily have enough knowledge of kind of standard distribution simply because from a personal standpoint, I kind of – I know that there's a – has been a necessity.

00:17:49.390 --> 00:17:51.670
 There's kind of this push to, like, say no to certain things.

00:17:51.670 --> 00:17:57.740
 Like, you kind of have to – like, you're going to say no more war, you know, no GMOs, for instance, or whatever it might be.

00:17:57.860 --> 00:18:02.380
 And that's – I totally, obviously, honor the people that are out there making that stand.

00:18:03.030 --> 00:18:08.630
 For me personally, I've just found myself really doing well in a place where I can just focus on the yes part.

00:18:08.630 --> 00:18:22.160
 And so ultimately, that's what we're doing here at Feed is simply looking at something that intuitively seems to be obvious because this is how – this just mirrors how life kind of functions on its own.

00:18:24.450 --> 00:18:38.250
 And so just basically believing that if we put a lot of energy and truthfulness into an endeavor that seems to be in and of its own right truthful in nature, that that's what life supports.

00:18:38.250 --> 00:18:48.000
 And so, yeah, I just think that as far as, like, big distribution goes, it happens in so many various different ways as far as, like, potentially not having as fresh food.

00:18:48.080 --> 00:18:53.660
 You know, it sits in cold storage for longer or it has to travel greater distances from growing regions that are super far away.

00:18:54.100 --> 00:19:09.330
 But ultimately, that's also – at this moment in time, that's still necessary because we don't even have the – even if we were to shut that off right now because we say, oh, it's inefficient, we wouldn't have enough food here, you know, without it.

00:19:09.450 --> 00:19:16.910
 So ultimately, I just think that it's where a lot of us are being called to just simply create that which is needed and focus on that.

00:19:17.050 --> 00:19:20.990
 And that sounds like a response to infrastructure in a certain way, right?

00:19:21.210 --> 00:19:23.230
 So affordable.

00:19:23.870 --> 00:19:27.090
 But it's because he buys from everybody, right?

00:19:27.210 --> 00:19:29.450
 From backyards, from huge farms.

00:19:29.670 --> 00:19:32.090
 I mean, he just buys a lot of produce.

00:19:32.370 --> 00:19:34.530
 And what does that look like as a business model?

00:19:34.570 --> 00:19:36.290
 Because it sounds like you kind of do that too.

00:19:36.490 --> 00:19:39.090
 Like, every week is a totally different week, right?

00:19:39.310 --> 00:19:39.990
 Yeah, for sure.

00:19:40.190 --> 00:19:46.300
 And maybe just to kind of keep that theme of financial viability as well, it does happen.

00:19:46.420 --> 00:20:00.010
 Obviously, we have – of those 60 farms, let's say, we might have the largest farm, which is, you know, farming on a total of maybe 115 acres, down to we have farms that we work with that might be farming on half an acre.

00:20:00.010 --> 00:20:15.950
 So, yeah, it's an incredible mix, which makes it incredibly rich, which also allows us to have an incredibly diverse list, which from a farm community that's trying to sell their food – this is kind of marketing without saying it, you know.

00:20:16.430 --> 00:20:24.750
 It's like it makes it a better sell when you have items on your list to a buyer that no one else has.

00:20:25.350 --> 00:20:36.290
 So, ultimately, yeah, it's challenging, of course, because on those half-acre farms, really, the only way that that can work on a wholesale level is if they're growing something that no one else has.

00:20:37.020 --> 00:20:41.440
 So, that's what we really try to focus on with farms of that scale.

00:20:41.920 --> 00:20:43.880
 Can you give me an example of how that works?

00:20:44.140 --> 00:20:44.640
 Yeah, for sure.

00:20:44.980 --> 00:20:48.430
 So, for instance, you know, let's face it.

00:20:48.430 --> 00:20:57.290
 In restaurants, like in the chef world or whatever it might be, even at the market, foods go through periods of being fads to a certain extent.

00:20:57.450 --> 00:21:00.450
 You know, things are really popular for a year or two, and then they kind of fall out of favor.

00:21:01.050 --> 00:21:04.250
 Well, that happens in the restaurants, obviously.

00:21:04.490 --> 00:21:13.110
 There's, like, certain items that for one or two years or three years, they might – all of a sudden, everybody might want it because of this new application that can be used or whatever.

00:21:13.130 --> 00:21:30.180
 And so – but a lot of times, what happens is whether it's, like, an heirloom bean or something like that, like a shelling bean, let's say, or even, like, a Romano-style bean or whatever it might be, something that needs to be trellised, there's a lot of hand labor that goes into that.

00:21:30.240 --> 00:21:36.830
 The harvest of something like that is really – it takes a lot of hours to pick, like, you know, five pounds of a product, basically.

00:21:37.270 --> 00:22:01.110
 So – but to that extent, what happens is, is usually on these farms of smaller scale, there's a lot more hand labor going into the crops, a lot more attention, which, if you believe in energetics of things, which I think most people that we do, I would say in our community, whether it's the farmers or the buyers, I would call all of us, even feed, I would call all of us artists to a certain extent.

00:22:01.190 --> 00:22:02.930
 We are practicing an art form.

00:22:03.410 --> 00:22:09.610
 And so when you see that, you know, it's like going to a museum and seeing a beautiful painting.

00:22:09.750 --> 00:22:19.830
 Well, when someone is giving all of their attention, their love, their energy to growing something because it's their passion, then that, I believe, gets felt by the plant.

00:22:19.910 --> 00:22:23.890
 You know, it receives that and potentially can show that in what it's producing.

00:22:24.490 --> 00:22:32.220
 And so, anyway, long story short is that, yeah, the smallest farms are usually focusing on growing maybe two or three items.

00:22:32.220 --> 00:22:44.720
 Like, we have – our smallest farm is literally – is growing two different types of Romano-style beans and a couple different types of baby cucumbers, and that's it.

00:22:45.000 --> 00:23:04.680
 But no one else grows them, and so basically, it's not an automatic thing, but when Marianne tells me what she has available in the field, it's almost guaranteed that it's going to sell because of the quantity not necessarily being that difficult to get out there to a community of buyers.

00:23:04.680 --> 00:23:11.960
 That's required feed to, you know, expand its buying power, you know, including more buyers in the mix, like adding another truck or whatever it might be.

00:23:12.610 --> 00:23:29.160
 But, you know, that seems to be a fairly successful model for a small-scale farm is focusing on a handful of items that are really heirloom artisanal quality that maybe not many other people grow, and then plugging those into a buying populace that is looking for that type of product.

00:23:29.160 --> 00:23:36.590
 Well, let's talk about how your business is really modeled around seeing diversity as an asset, right?

00:23:36.650 --> 00:23:42.980
 So you have a huge range of clients, like, I think, right, from – who are they?

00:23:42.980 --> 00:23:50.340
 And then also, like, how do you select farmers, or how do they select you, and both ends of that.

00:23:50.500 --> 00:23:57.080
 And then I'd also like to hear about what a diversity of income streams means for this business working.

00:23:58.520 --> 00:24:00.210
 Yeah, let's see.

00:24:00.560 --> 00:24:18.120
 I would say that as far as, like, our buying populace, like our client base, it's probably 80% restaurants, maybe 85% restaurants, another 5% being caterers, and another 10% being markets.

00:24:18.120 --> 00:24:25.210
 But in those market clientele, it's mostly, like, the boutique markets, like the Buy Right or Good Earth in Fairfax or Monterey Market.

00:24:26.010 --> 00:24:30.530
 We also do have a good presence with Oliver's here in Sonoma County, and I do see that growing.

00:24:30.750 --> 00:24:36.950
 They're definitely making a point of wanting to focus on supporting local farms a lot more.

00:24:37.410 --> 00:24:46.380
 So that would only work in an economy like we have in the Bay Area, where there's a lot of high-end food buyers and a lot of farms.

00:24:47.220 --> 00:24:50.340
 Do you see this model working in other places in the world?

00:24:51.130 --> 00:24:57.680
 Yeah, I mean, I think that it, I think that it, once again, I think that it has a long way to go here in the Bay Area.

00:24:57.840 --> 00:25:10.040
 Like, I think the Bay Area is a perfect place for that, because, and I think that it can work in other areas, because I believe that, so first off, our society has been built up relatively inefficiently.

00:25:10.120 --> 00:25:19.540
 You have, like, these huge population-dense urban areas that may or may not have the ability to even be able to have food brought to them.

00:25:19.540 --> 00:25:24.260
 Like, let's say a city like Phoenix, let's say it's like a million people living in the desert, you know, kind of inefficient.

00:25:24.620 --> 00:25:26.380
 You know, nothing against them, of course.

00:25:26.760 --> 00:25:34.110
 But even an area like San Francisco, where you might have, you know, 800,000 or 900,000 people living in San Francisco proper.

00:25:34.570 --> 00:25:40.790
 But once again, there's no way that you could grow that food within the city limits just because of the lack of resources.

00:25:40.790 --> 00:25:47.380
 So I do feel that it may not work in every area, but I do see it working in a lot of areas.

00:25:47.520 --> 00:25:53.380
 And I do, it's always, it's going to be necessary in the Bay Area because of, one, topography.

00:25:53.770 --> 00:25:56.030
 So you look at Sonoma County and do the topography.

00:25:56.570 --> 00:26:04.760
 It's really, it would be really challenging to have a lot of really big row cropping farms here because of the limited resource of, like, flat, you know, land, basically.

00:26:05.000 --> 00:26:07.420
 There's, especially here in West County, it's very hilly.

00:26:08.360 --> 00:26:10.640
 But also then you have the cost of the land, too.

00:26:10.640 --> 00:26:20.530
 And that's going to obviously dictate the ability of a vegetable farm to exist because the revenues per row foot simply don't allow big land purchases by young farmers.

00:26:20.710 --> 00:26:31.940
 So, yeah, I do believe that this type of model is actually really necessary in these areas where cost of land is high and topography may not lend itself to large scale farming.

00:26:31.940 --> 00:26:43.070
 And I think that that probably exists in a lot of outlying urban areas, you know, in suburban areas, basically, where there's still population, but there's enough space to where at least you could have small farmers.

00:26:43.290 --> 00:26:59.770
 And once again, I just, I also feel that it ultimately becomes a lot more efficient because of the bare bones infrastructure that we were talking about before, where I think that resources can be used in a more wise manner.

00:27:00.190 --> 00:27:05.700
 You know, I don't think that we need to go think about reinventing the wheel and creating brand new systems.

00:27:05.700 --> 00:27:14.900
 I think that, like we were talking about before we started recording, I think it's really more about how do we utilize the resources that we do have, the infrastructure that we do have.

00:27:15.010 --> 00:27:20.720
 In a more efficient manner that ultimately leads to a ridiculous amount of abundance.

00:27:20.960 --> 00:27:26.740
 I mean, I think if you look at it, you've been to the farm markets, you know, farmers, I'm sure it's the food that's on your table at home.

00:27:28.060 --> 00:27:34.110
 You've seen that if that was the only food available, it's amazingly beautiful.

00:27:34.310 --> 00:27:35.510
 It's amazingly vibrant.

00:27:35.510 --> 00:27:45.050
 And I think that's really important for people to understand that the baseline of sustainability in a micro region like the Bay Area or Sonoma County is ridiculously abundant.

00:27:45.310 --> 00:27:47.450
 I mean, that's all I can say about that.

00:27:47.550 --> 00:27:54.910
 I mean, it's going to lend itself to having the most amazing – people can't even imagine how good the food system would be if they actually just dedicated a little energy to it.

00:27:54.910 --> 00:28:02.070
 Well, when I hear you talk about that, I think about – well, I went through a whole – there's a whole lot of thoughts.

00:28:02.170 --> 00:28:16.180
 One of them was disasters and food security and thinking about these local hubs and places for distribution that are really different than something like Safeway, for example.

00:28:17.410 --> 00:28:28.710
 And I think in a certain way that are even different than something like the farmer's market that happens once a week but usually in a public place and there's not that much infrastructure around it.

00:28:28.830 --> 00:28:46.530
 So, to me, when I hear you talk about who your clients are and kind of like where you think things go, I kind of see the wheels turning in your head of what would be your wildest dream of how you would feed your community the system that you work on?

00:28:46.790 --> 00:28:47.270
 Yeah, for sure.

00:28:47.830 --> 00:29:00.330
 And also, just to continue on that, honoring the current infrastructure piece, like you mentioned Safeway, for instance, that's like a really big piece is how do we – I mean, we have cold storage, for instance, in all these markets.

00:29:01.100 --> 00:29:05.710
 You know, how do we just try to encourage them to do a better job of sourcing?

00:29:06.170 --> 00:29:12.740
 And I think ultimately a lot of that comes from the public, you know, demanding that to be available, basically.

00:29:13.140 --> 00:29:14.380
 So, there's that aspect.

00:29:15.260 --> 00:29:16.740
 What do you see that looking like?

00:29:17.300 --> 00:29:25.820
 Oh, well, I basically – I mean, this is a complicated issue because a lot of times it just simply comes down to economics.

00:29:26.000 --> 00:29:33.410
 I think that in a consumer-based society, which is what we are, consumer-centric, everything is based on price, really.

00:29:33.550 --> 00:29:35.550
 I mean, that's like usually the underlying factor.

00:29:35.980 --> 00:29:39.840
 Quality, those types of things don't necessarily weigh as heavily as price.

00:29:40.280 --> 00:29:48.750
 And so, ultimately, I think that, as you know, here in Sonoma County, the cost of goods is really high, you know, with the land price and the cost of labor and all that stuff.

00:29:48.970 --> 00:29:49.950
 And, you know, that's fine.

00:29:49.990 --> 00:29:50.670
 It is what it is.

00:29:51.130 --> 00:30:02.650
 But I think that ultimately when people decide to move to a place like Sonoma County or live in the Bay Area, knowing that you have to spend a certain amount of money to just sit at the – you know, you got to pay this ante just to buy the property if you're going to buy the house or whatever.

00:30:02.650 --> 00:30:04.050
 That's if you can afford to buy the house.

00:30:04.730 --> 00:30:13.880
 That ultimately there needs to be, as a culture, as a society, kind of a willingness to understand that there's all – it continues.

00:30:14.060 --> 00:30:15.980
 You don't just buy the house and say you've done your part.

00:30:16.690 --> 00:30:19.870
 You know, it continues with investing.

00:30:20.480 --> 00:30:25.520
 You know, I say that with my fingers up, like in quotes, investing in your farm community simply by buying the food from the farmers.

00:30:25.720 --> 00:30:36.770
 So I think that in a lot of ways it's a societal shift because I'm sure that markets out there, even Safeway, for instance, I'm sure that they would carry a ton more locally grown food if they knew that it would sell.

00:30:37.750 --> 00:30:40.330
 But it's, you know, it's a risk for them because of the price.

00:30:40.410 --> 00:30:42.870
 The price is higher and most people aren't adjusted to that yet.

00:30:42.870 --> 00:30:49.880
 So somehow – and I do believe it's through education and storytelling, like what you guys are doing – it's a really important method.

00:30:50.020 --> 00:30:56.070
 I mean, it kind of goes hand in hand with making the food available to buyers in an easier fashion.

00:30:56.750 --> 00:31:00.770
 But yeah, and then as far as, like, kind of how would I envision that happening?

00:31:00.890 --> 00:31:05.070
 Like what's – and then also to go back to your – the prior question, which was what was my wildest dream?

00:31:05.210 --> 00:31:16.710
 I mean, I think that one of the main focuses of Feed Sonoma is also to potentially be able to create a replicable model, you know, thinking that there's probably going to be a core set of values for these types of communities.

00:31:17.590 --> 00:31:36.000
 If you have a community of farms, like on our scale, like the San Francisco Bay Area with all these different factors that determine the size of a farm potentially and where they are, there's most likely going to be a core set of values that every community needs to have in place, you know, to be able to achieve this idea of aggregation and distribution to represent the farmers.

00:31:36.000 --> 00:31:40.880
 So that the farmers – and the point of that being so the farmers can stay on the farm if that's what they want to do.

00:31:41.320 --> 00:31:49.220
 But yeah, and then knowing that every other community, even though there might be this core set of values, there's also going to be some particular unique features based on whatever.

00:31:49.360 --> 00:31:50.300
 It might be microclimates.

00:31:50.400 --> 00:31:51.640
 It might be farmer personalities.

00:31:52.060 --> 00:31:53.700
 It might be the size of the farm.

00:31:53.820 --> 00:31:56.480
 It might be what they're – you know, just what they're growing basically.

00:31:57.580 --> 00:32:01.680
 But that a model like Feed Sonoma could work in other communities.

00:32:01.860 --> 00:32:03.380
 And so that's definitely on the radar.

00:32:03.380 --> 00:32:20.300
 I mean, not from any sort of ownership or, you know, franchise modality or anything like that, but simply believing that if this model works, then I could see myself or us helping other communities to create a similar model.

00:32:20.300 --> 00:32:30.630
 Also, knowing that if we have more organizations like this working together, it only makes the movement, if you will, or the effect of how we're actually evolving the food system that much stronger.

00:32:31.770 --> 00:32:35.790
 And what do you see for consumers who just can't afford this kind of food?

00:32:36.110 --> 00:32:39.210
 I mean, how do you see that fitting into a model like this?

00:32:39.330 --> 00:32:40.450
 Yeah, it's a total conundrum.

00:32:40.790 --> 00:32:46.930
 I mean, it's a dilemma right now because of that – just the basic economics of it.

00:32:47.690 --> 00:32:55.470
 If the cost of good is high, the farmer has to make a certain amount of money on their crop or else they can't pay their bills basically.

00:32:56.330 --> 00:32:59.900
 And yeah, it's not a just world, you know.

00:33:00.380 --> 00:33:02.380
 I'm not sure what the solutions are.

00:33:02.640 --> 00:33:12.040
 I heard in one of your prior podcasts with Joey Smith at Let's Go Farm, his idea about, you know, promoting farms to dedicate to food bank situations.

00:33:12.460 --> 00:33:16.560
 And – but still, that's not even addressing the in-between, you know.

00:33:16.700 --> 00:33:19.010
 And yeah, it's not an easy question.

00:33:19.550 --> 00:33:27.820
 It's – I think that ultimately it may – the burden in that regard from a societal standpoint may not fall solely upon the farms.

00:33:27.820 --> 00:33:43.290
 I think that ultimately that – because reasons for that happening are so varied, a lot of times in those types of environments people might not even necessarily have or think that they have the time to even cook meals.

00:33:44.480 --> 00:33:59.700
 And so, you know, due to maybe two parents working full-time jobs and having to commute and all that stuff that's happening and getting home and the kids have to do homework and it's just like, oh, let's just grab that thing from Trader Joe's and stick it in the microwave or whatever it is, you know.

00:34:00.180 --> 00:34:14.930
 And so, it's such a multi-level dilemma that I think that trying to figure it out simply within the farm community would be an injustice to the farms, basically, because they still have to pay their bills.

00:34:15.810 --> 00:34:18.530
 So, basically, what I'm saying is I don't have the answer for that.

00:34:18.750 --> 00:34:23.240
 Like, I can't even begin to fathom what a solution would look like in 10 years.

00:34:23.940 --> 00:34:37.260
 But I do know that the folks that can support the local food system that do have the means to do it, we need to basically be able to ensure – we need to be able to tell the local farm community that you're going to sell everything that you're going to grow that's worthy of selling.

00:34:39.640 --> 00:34:40.140
 Excuse me.

00:34:40.510 --> 00:34:46.320
 We need to be able to tell our farm community that that is possible, to be able to give them the confidence that they're going to survive.

00:34:47.940 --> 00:35:05.740
 And then from that confidence, from that foundation of financial sustainability, then we can call on the next generation of farmers and then we can start invoking potential next-step solutions to those types of dilemmas, getting fresh food to the, you know, quote-unquote food deserts that are out there where people don't have access to that type of food.

00:35:06.100 --> 00:35:17.840
 But once again, it's like there's so many different factors coming into play, you know, people trying to achieve a lot in life and not having the time to even spend with their family or, like we were talking about, not even having the time to cook.

00:35:17.940 --> 00:35:27.680
 And so, there's so many factors that get put in to how that gets created, how that problem gets created that I definitely do not have an answer to that question.

00:35:27.780 --> 00:35:28.780
 I really honestly don't.

00:35:28.840 --> 00:35:35.440
 I just know that we need to at least ensure the current farm community at least be able to give them an opportunity to survive.

00:35:35.440 --> 00:35:37.910
 Well, so let's talk about that a little bit.

00:35:38.050 --> 00:35:58.180
 Here, we're having, in Sonoma County, as you probably heard a little bit through Katie, and I'm sure lots of the farmers that you work with, people have a hard time having access to land and having a guarantee that these businesses and farms that they invest in can last, right?

00:35:58.300 --> 00:35:59.340
 It's too expensive.

00:36:01.180 --> 00:36:11.220
 So how does Feed Sonoma, or what do you think about that, like in terms of making models that ensure that we do have farmers in our communities?

00:36:11.550 --> 00:36:16.540
 Yeah, it's a great question because it's actually ultimately Feed Sonoma is nothing if there's no farms.

00:36:16.800 --> 00:36:32.080
 I mean, obviously, we already have a more challenging time in the winter because simple fact is there's not as much food being grown in Sonoma County in the winter as there is in October or, you know, July, August, whatever.

00:36:33.990 --> 00:36:45.360
 So, yeah, once again, that's another quandary for sure, and I think that that's an issue that's being talked about a lot right now in our community as far as land access goes.

00:36:46.990 --> 00:37:03.630
 I think that it's going to be a group effort, basically looking at organizations like the Sonoma Land Trust, potentially being able to use some of the land that they are protecting for to be able to actually have farmers on the land producing food and revenues there.

00:37:04.590 --> 00:37:24.110
 I think, but you're talking about a huge dilemma where you have a community, we're having a new younger population of farmers coming in now because obviously the generation of farmers that actually own their land, it's kind of disappearing because just simply because of age.

00:37:24.450 --> 00:37:38.780
 And most farmers nowadays are having to lease their land from a landowner that that particular person might be totally fired up and obviously in it, you know, 100% wanting to support that farm and have them on their land.

00:37:38.860 --> 00:37:50.880
 I mean, of course, they're leasing it already to them, but life can happen, you know, happens to anybody where even the best intention might, for whatever unforeseen reason, might have to sell their land, let's say.

00:37:51.400 --> 00:37:56.600
 And when that happens, maybe the new owner comes in and doesn't necessarily want that same farm to be in existence.

00:37:56.900 --> 00:38:01.120
 And so, yeah, it's a real problem where most young farmers now don't own their land.

00:38:01.220 --> 00:38:03.040
 They're having to lease it from someone else.

00:38:03.040 --> 00:38:15.920
 And so this is just another aspect of our environment nowadays where it's going to take now a new culture of landowners who become stewards.

00:38:16.840 --> 00:38:26.610
 And so folks that look at an area like Sonoma County where there is still open space and there is still, you know, potentially acreage, not only as an investment.

00:38:28.270 --> 00:38:31.450
 From a financial standpoint, but as an investment in their community.

00:38:31.490 --> 00:38:33.530
 And so I believe it's possible.

00:38:33.530 --> 00:38:52.180
 Like, I totally believe it's possible for folks because I believe that there's a young population now that has the financial means in the San Francisco Bay Area that not only are making a good living in that regard monetarily, but that are also relatively more plugged into what is happening with the evolution of our society as well.

00:38:52.980 --> 00:38:58.760
 And so, yeah, I mean, you see yourself yourself as a conduit between those populations.

00:38:59.070 --> 00:39:00.850
 Yeah, I would like to think so.

00:39:00.950 --> 00:39:08.470
 I mean, I think ultimately with feed, we've you know, you get to a certain point where you get a little bit more efficient, you know, as a business, let's say.

00:39:08.470 --> 00:39:13.600
 And you have a wonderful staff on board that is making things work really well.

00:39:13.780 --> 00:39:25.370
 And it's not just one employee anymore and allows us to begin to be involved a little bit more with some of those bigger picture items, you know, potentially like a policy directive.

00:39:25.370 --> 00:39:49.770
 And, you know, not necessarily from potentially spearheading it most likely, but I definitely do see it as being a conduit of, you know, bringing people together that because we have such a dynamic community already that where we can bring those types of people together that then begin mingling with the folks in our communities that are maybe like a nonprofit or an NGO that that is 100 percent.

00:39:49.770 --> 00:39:57.770
 Their task is to be involved with the policy of, you know, protecting land or whatever it might be, or water rights or all that stuff that needs to happen.

00:39:58.070 --> 00:40:02.790
 But, yeah, I definitely see us being a really important connector in that regard, for sure.

00:40:02.790 --> 00:40:12.740
 Well, I think maybe we should wrap it up in thinking about, like, do you think that making a livelihood is an act of advocacy for you?

00:40:13.810 --> 00:40:25.360
 Yeah. Yeah. Yeah. We were talking about this before, too. And this also came from your conversation with Joey Smith talking about being a he was talking about spending time as a farmer and spending time as an advocate.

00:40:25.360 --> 00:40:39.620
 And, yeah, I mean, that was I really appreciated those words because it made me realize that generally in our in our community of folks that we are involved with and the people here at feed simply by doing our work.

00:40:39.620 --> 00:40:56.040
 We have become advocates, meaning that every the intention that I have brought to feed every single day since I started has been basically to steward something for my children and for all children.

00:40:56.040 --> 00:41:10.020
 But for my children, you know, when I look my kids in the eye, I like I cannot imagine looking my kids, my children in the eye and not wanting to go out every day and be able to tell them that I'm helping to create something that was better than we found it.

00:41:10.100 --> 00:41:13.410
 And that we're going to give that to them, you know, as a food system, basically.

00:41:13.910 --> 00:41:30.740
 And so, yeah, I think that when we're talking about, you know, feed helping farmers sell more food, you know, feed helping farmers become financially sustainable, you know, feed connecting people in the food community with, you know, an organization that might be working on, you know, increasing land access.

00:41:30.740 --> 00:41:51.870
 And, you know, every single aspect of what we're doing, you know, creating a more efficient food system, you know, figuring out how to utilize infrastructure in a more efficient manner, you know, creating a baseline of sustainability that's ridiculously abundant, you know, saying yes to a ecologically sound way of farming, which is, you know, saying no to GMOs and all that type of stuff.

00:41:51.870 --> 00:41:58.090
 But by doing all those acts, I mean, that's 100% being an advocate for life, basically.

00:41:58.750 --> 00:42:05.430
 And, you know, once again, I think we all come from similar seed, if not the same seed.

00:42:06.280 --> 00:42:09.230
 And so we all have our roots in the same same earth.

00:42:09.490 --> 00:42:11.130
 You know, we're all here together.

00:42:11.130 --> 00:42:18.200
 Like nobody's more blessed than anybody else, per se, or nobody's more deserving than anybody else, per se.

00:42:18.200 --> 00:42:26.750
 So I think that when we devote ourselves to a foundational element, such as food, like there's air, food, water.

00:42:26.910 --> 00:42:30.090
 I mean, we could talk about others, too, obviously, community, love, and all those.

00:42:30.210 --> 00:42:37.230
 But air, food, and water, when you devote yourself to a foundational element, you become an advocate, basically.

00:42:37.570 --> 00:42:39.230
 And I think that's what we're doing now.

00:42:39.290 --> 00:42:42.420
 That's kind of the current generation is how do we create business?

00:42:42.590 --> 00:42:44.750
 Because we need to, not because I want to.

00:42:45.420 --> 00:42:51.590
 Like I don't really give a rip about the business aspect of it, like the money part and all that stuff.

00:42:51.590 --> 00:42:54.850
 But I'm more than happy to figure out a solution that makes that work.

00:42:54.910 --> 00:43:06.870
 But how do we create business that can allow for this evolution to happen where business mirrors that organic biological function?

00:43:07.730 --> 00:43:10.190
 And so that's kind of really what this is.

00:43:10.230 --> 00:43:11.690
 It's like we're a case study, of course.

00:43:11.770 --> 00:43:13.050
 We don't know that this is going to work.

00:43:13.330 --> 00:43:13.810
 None of us do.

00:43:14.070 --> 00:43:14.950
 You know, none of the farmers do.

00:43:15.300 --> 00:43:15.760
 None of us do.

00:43:15.860 --> 00:43:18.170
 I mean, all the time it feels like we're on a knife edge, you know.

00:43:18.490 --> 00:43:19.710
 It's a little bit pointy up here.

00:43:20.800 --> 00:43:24.450
 So, I mean, it's a constant case study, but it's also really exciting.

00:43:25.140 --> 00:43:26.060
 I mean, you get fired up.

00:43:26.100 --> 00:43:26.740
 I get fired up.

00:43:26.820 --> 00:43:33.600
 It's, you know, it's really exciting knowing that we are, in our subtle way, we're evolving how things happen on the planet.

00:43:33.700 --> 00:43:39.690
 And I do believe that I can wake up in the morning and look my children in the eye and say that we're doing it.

00:43:39.770 --> 00:43:45.670
 I mean, I feel like we are creating something that is going to be better and more vibrant than the way we found it.

00:43:45.810 --> 00:43:47.350
 And we all want to hit the home run.

00:43:47.550 --> 00:43:52.280
 You know, we all want to have, like, 100% locally sustainable food system and make that happen.

00:43:52.580 --> 00:43:54.040
 I mean, that's great.

00:43:54.080 --> 00:43:54.460
 It's awesome.

00:43:54.720 --> 00:43:56.000
 I mean, that's the indigenous dream.

00:43:56.160 --> 00:43:57.020
 We have that right.

00:43:57.100 --> 00:43:57.740
 We're all indigenous.

00:43:59.050 --> 00:44:00.740
 But it's also going to take some time.

00:44:01.130 --> 00:44:05.740
 You know, I think enlightenment, that can happen in a snap of a finger.

00:44:06.040 --> 00:44:07.970
 You know, all of a sudden it's like, whoa, I got it.

00:44:08.230 --> 00:44:09.270
 And it's always in you.

00:44:09.330 --> 00:44:10.090
 Like, you can feel it.

00:44:10.190 --> 00:44:15.330
 You know, you sat underneath that Bodhi tree and, you know, you feel enlightened.

00:44:15.450 --> 00:44:26.970
 But it's also to enact that type of change in a brick and mortar world, in the physical world, especially one in which the constructs may not exactly make sense, you know, from that type of conscious sustainability.

00:44:27.410 --> 00:44:28.610
 It's like it's going to take some time.

00:44:29.000 --> 00:44:37.180
 So and especially because we're it really does feel like knowing that our resources are so scarce now and limited in order to honor those.

00:44:37.180 --> 00:44:40.200
 It makes the most sense to work with the current infrastructure.

00:44:40.360 --> 00:44:41.320
 And that's going to take time, too.

00:44:41.700 --> 00:44:43.620
 They're not all made out of silly putty.

00:44:44.140 --> 00:44:45.340
 That's why they call it brick and mortar.

00:44:45.980 --> 00:44:47.260
 Well, thanks so much, Tim.

00:44:47.320 --> 00:44:48.820
 It's been great having you on the show today.

00:44:49.230 --> 00:44:50.130
 Yeah, thank you so much.

00:44:50.210 --> 00:45:00.750
 And really, I just want to say thank you from all of our community, too, because this ultimately feed is only here because of the farms and because of the folks that are out there buying the food from the farm.

00:45:00.890 --> 00:45:04.270
 So really, I mean, you're saying thank you to a whole lot of people.

00:45:04.470 --> 00:45:06.570
 So from them, I say thank you as well.

